[size=4][b]How It Works[/b][/size]

If you are new to this kind of mod, the idea is very simple:

[list]
[*]The [b]Mod Loader[/b] lets StarRupture load plugin mods[/*]
[*]This plugin runs while your game is open[/*]
[*]It exposes local data on your own PC[/*]
[*]In dedicated-server play, the client and server builds of [b]MapExtension_Plugin[/b] exchange map and rupture data with each other[/*]
[*][b]MapExtensionViewer.html[/b] reads that local data and displays it in your browser[/*]
[/list]

This is [b]not[/b] an online service and [b]not[/b] a separate game client. It is just a local viewer for your current game session.

[line]

[size=4][b]What You Will See[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Where your cargo buildings are[/*]
[*]Which cargo buildings are connected together[/*]
[*]Which items are travelling through those links[/*]
[*]Where teleporters are placed[/*]
[*]Where players are located[/*]
[*]Where abandoned bases and observed available plants are located[/*]
[*]Consistent colors for Hydrobulb, Polifruit, Oxallop, Purplant, Serpent Root, Prickler, Prism Herb, and Sulheart[/*]
[*]The current rupture-cycle timeline in detailed or compact mode[/*]
[/list]

Plant coverage grows as players visit or otherwise load World Partition areas, and discovered plants are remembered on disk per save session so coverage survives game restarts. In solo/local play, the in-game MapExtension panel can also scan the calibrated map bounds progressively, one zone at a time. The operation is cancellable but may take several minutes and cause strong temporary slowdowns.

This is especially useful when your base grows and you want a cleaner overview of your layout, logistics lines, and rupture activity.

[line]

[size=4][b]Solo And Dedicated Server Support[/b][/size]

The plugin works in both solo and multiplayer, but rupture data is not sourced the same way in every case.

[list]
[*][b]Solo / local-host:[/b] the plugin can read rupture data locally from the running game[/*]
[*][b]Dedicated server:[/b] install the server build of [b]MapExtension_Plugin[/b] on the server so the client and server plugins can exchange map, POI, and rupture data[/*]
[/list]

The map viewer runs on the client. In dedicated-server play, the client plugin communicates with the server plugin, and the server plugin sends map, POI, and rupture updates back to the client plugin.

[b]Important:[/b] dedicated-server synchronization uses protocol v4 and requires matching protocol versions. Update the client and dedicated-server builds together; a mixed-version pair ignores incompatible packets and cannot synchronize a remote snapshot.

